    /// bases of the declaration of a class or interface; an instantiation of a generic one (`Props<string>`, only a
    /// reference) has none of its own, so they are read from the generic type it instantiates, which names the same
    /// types over its own type parameters. An object type that is neither (a type literal, a mapped type) has none.
    /// `None` for any other type (a union, a type parameter): whether it has bases is not for the checker to say here.
    /// A traversal that keeps the arguments of what it finds ([`Self::base_layers`]) cannot read them this way, since
    /// `HTMLAttributes<T>` is not `HTMLAttributes<HTMLDivElement>`, and tests [`states_bases`] of the type itself.
